    a: "They build safety behaviour into the model — refusing harmful requests, resisting manipulation. Those are real and worth having. But they know nothing about your business, so they can't stop an assistant emailing the wrong client or quoting an outdated price."
  - q: "Isn't a good system prompt enough?"
    a: "It's the weakest layer, though still worth having. Instructions can be argued with, forgotten in a long conversation, or overridden by text the model reads. Access limits can't be — if the assistant was never granted the folder, no instruction gets it there."
  - q: "What's the single most effective guardrail?"
    a: "A human approving anything irreversible. Drafting, summarising and suggesting are safe to automate; sending, paying, publishing and deleting are not. That one boundary prevents most real-world harm."
  - q: "Do guardrails make AI less useful?"
    a: "Badly designed ones do — if every action needs three approvals, people route around the system. Good ones are invisible in normal use and only bite at the edges. Scope tightly, approve narrowly."
